1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:04,529
A supermarket store order is a hundred-plus cases.

2
00:00:04,579 --> 00:00:08,247
The warehouse system already sends them heaviest first.

3
00:00:08,297 --> 00:00:12,099
What it never tells a new picker is where each case goes.

4
00:00:12,149 --> 00:00:16,290
Get layer one wrong, and eighty cases later, the pallet leans.

5
00:00:17,240 --> 00:00:20,375
This is Stack Right, a pallet trainer for Specs.

6
00:00:20,425 --> 00:00:22,100
You're on the pallet jack.

7
00:00:22,150 --> 00:00:26,414
Grab whatever's glowing, and a ghost shows exactly where it goes,

8
00:00:26,464 --> 00:00:27,276
at real size.

9
00:00:27,326 --> 00:00:30,660
The tag gives the layer, the count, and the weight.

10
00:00:30,710 --> 00:00:33,580
Drop it near the ghost, and it snaps square.

11
00:00:35,930 --> 00:00:38,990
Hang it over the edge, and it slides back with the reason.

12
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:44,410
Leave a gap, and it tells you that too.

13
00:00:47,070 --> 00:00:49,183
Every mistake is counted, by rule.

14
00:00:49,233 --> 00:00:52,556
The next one lights up as soon as the last one lands,

15
00:00:52,606 --> 00:00:54,910
so there's only ever one thing to do.

16
00:00:58,770 --> 00:01:00,422
Real freight isn't tidy.

17
00:01:00,472 --> 00:01:04,110
Sacks, cartons, trays of tins, and the odd long one.

18
00:01:05,160 --> 00:01:06,809
Layer three mixes sizes.

19
00:01:06,859 --> 00:01:08,862
The big water cases go first,

20
00:01:08,912 --> 00:01:11,765
and the juice fills the strip they leave.

21
00:01:11,815 --> 00:01:16,296
It's turned ninety degrees, so the joints cross the layer below.

22
00:01:16,346 --> 00:01:18,137
None of it is hand-placed.

23
00:01:18,187 --> 00:01:21,040
The plan is computed from the case sizes.

24
00:01:22,090 --> 00:01:24,539
Real layers don't always come out even.

25
00:01:24,589 --> 00:01:27,229
There's only enough soup for half a layer,

26
00:01:27,279 --> 00:01:29,856
so it goes at the back and leaves a step.

27
00:01:29,906 --> 00:01:34,020
The trays of tins fill the front, two high, and it's level again.

28
00:01:36,560 --> 00:01:40,601
I pick orders in a warehouse, so I built this from my own shifts.

29
00:01:40,651 --> 00:01:44,439
Clad's plan-lens skill turned my answers into a design brief.

30
00:01:44,489 --> 00:01:45,950
I corrected its premise,

31
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:48,970
because real orders already come heaviest first.

32
00:01:49,020 --> 00:01:52,432
Then Clad built the lens through the Lens Studio M C P,

33
00:01:52,482 --> 00:01:55,830
tested by a self-test driver and seven Leaf scenarios.

34
00:01:57,180 --> 00:01:59,092
Thirty-four items, five layers,

35
00:01:59,142 --> 00:02:02,572
a hundred and forty centimetres, under the height line.

36
00:02:02,622 --> 00:02:04,280
Time, and mistakes by rule.

37
00:02:05,430 --> 00:02:07,789
Real orders are three times this size.

38
00:02:07,839 --> 00:02:10,768
Stack Right lets a new picker build them right,

39
00:02:10,818 --> 00:02:12,606
before they touch real stock.

40
00:02:12,656 --> 00:02:15,712
It's built and tested in the Lens Studio preview.

41
00:02:15,762 --> 00:02:17,550
Next is a real pair of Specs.
